From: Veron
Subject: [PATCH] cc_macro: a function-like macro name without `(` is not a call

C expands a function-like macro only when its name is followed by an open
paren. Anywhere else the name is an ordinary identifier and must be left
alone. maybe_expand() ate the name FIRST and then checked, so every bare use
was a hard error:

    libtcc.c:267: Function-like-macro called without parameter list

Measured by .github/workflows/m2-normal-mode-gap.yml, which compiles our
patched tcc in NORMAL mode -- the mode we will actually build tcc in, as
opposed to --bootstrap-mode, which is a reduced language we only ever used
because M2-Planet's own source is written to fit it.

The fix checks for `(` BEFORE eating the name and, when it is absent, returns
exactly as the "not a macro at all" path above already does.

VERIFIED, not argued: with this applied, M2-Planet's output over its own nine
source files is BYTE-IDENTICAL to the unpatched compiler's, so nothing that
was compiling before compiles differently. A bare macro name now reports
"X is not a defined symbol" -- the correct diagnostic for an undefined
identifier -- instead of dying in the preprocessor.

This is a conformance fix, not a feature: it makes M2-Planet agree with C
where it previously did not.
@@ -991,6 +991,21 @@
 		return token->next;
 	}
 
+	/* A FUNCTION-LIKE MACRO NAME NOT FOLLOWED BY `(` IS NOT AN INVOCATION.
+	 * C says such a name is left as an ordinary identifier -- it is only a
+	 * macro call when the next token is an open paren. This used to eat the
+	 * name first and then die on the check below, so any bare use of the name
+	 * -- passing it somewhere, or reusing the spelling as a declarator -- was
+	 * a hard error. tcc trips it at libtcc.c:267. Returning here is the same
+	 * thing the `not a macro at all` path above already does. */
+	if(NULL != hold->arguments)
+	{
+		if(token->next->s[0] != '(')
+		{
+			return token->next;
+		}
+	}
+
 	token = eat_token(token);
 
 	struct token_list* expansion = hold->expansion;
